# DivideIntegers and RemainderOfIntegers choose their code by the told ranges of dividend and divisor: no test for nought where the divisor is told never to be nought, none for the least int32 by -1, narrow forms.
# A range that is false would trap there. Under CheckProofsAtRunTime both operands are held to their ranges where the operation stands, and the process stops by name.
# The stops are counted: the walk's own checks of what it has proven in the function (six in shared, two in parted) and one for the operation. A plain build and CheckBoundsAtRunTime have none of them.
# f1 = shared, f2 = parted
# RUN(SHARED): emit f1:Body mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# SHARED-COUNT-7: effects = Terminal
# RUN(PARTED): emit f2:Body mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# PARTED-COUNT-3: effects = Terminal
# RUN(PLAIN): emit f1:Body mechanisms=all
# PLAIN-NOT: effects = Terminal
# RUN(POSITIONS): emit f2:Body mechanisms=all testing=CheckBoundsAtRunTime
# POSITIONS-NOT: effects = Terminal
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# ANSWERS: 33 8
